# Gate review — M-next D9: the version-1 lift as a pure encode, and the fixture regeneration

## Verdict: **READY**

The ruled slice is implemented as the header ruling (1791231000000) and r3 §1.8/D9 specify it, the ruled test rows are all present and pinned, the self-check is load-bearing on every lift path, and the three-host evidence is coherent. I found no correctness defect in the lift itself. One contract gap survives in the canonical ordering (finding 1) and two carried items (the §8 widening, the doc text) still need the architect's disposition — the engineer already flagged both. Landing condition: finding 1 is fixed in this diff (a one-line `ordered` wrap) or recorded beside the engineer's §11.6 residual in the same sign-off that disposes the widening. Nothing else blocks.

What I did: read the engineer's report, the header ruling, D plan r3 (§1.8, the D-slice table, the D9/D10/D11 test contracts) and astra finding 8; read the full diff of all nine modified files and the two new test files in context; read `handoff.cljc`, `holder/export.cljc` and `checkpoint.cljc` whole where the diff demanded it. Nothing edited, no suite run; where I cite outcomes I either verified them in the code or say I am taking them from the engineer's logs, several of which I inspected directly (`target/d9-flutter-…lift-v1-test.log` all 18 pass; `d9-shadow-test.log` 0 failures/0 errors, 0 warnings; `d9-kondo.log` 0/0; `d9-digests.log` first-park `d94ab55d…` matching the new pin; `d9-gen-ledger.log` wrote 32046 bytes = the new `ledger-v1.txt` size exactly).

## Findings

**1. (Medium — the one item to disposition) Module-store maps escape the version-1 canonical order.** r3 §1.8: "Maps and sets, and anything derived from them such as cell numbering, are ordered by canonical encoded key bytes." The slice orders literal maps (`handoff.cljc:342-348`), sets (`:348`), envs (`:379`), frames (`:392`), the parked table (`:925-927`) and the task store (`:928-930`) — but `snapshot-module-stores` (`handoff.cljc:750-762`) still walks each module's store map in host iteration order, and `(encode v)` there mints cells through `cell-for!` (`:206-214`). A task whose one module store holds two or more cursor-bearing values gets host-dependent cell ids (`c-N` by `count` of `:cells`), so different bytes per host under version 1. Latent today — no fixture reaches it, and a single cell-minting entry is order-independent — and it is strictly narrower than the D8 hole this slice closes, but it is the same class and the contract sentence covers it. One `ordered` wrap (the task-store line shows the shape) plus ideally a row closes it; alternatively record it beside the engineer's §11.6 residual for the architect's nod. The engineer's §11.6 flags a different residual (aliased markers as literal keys), not this one.

**2. (Low) The lift's own refusals on an install child carry no `:yin.k/path`.** `unliftable-hold` answers bare `{:yin.k/hold …}` (`handoff.cljc:835-836`); `enter` names the task path (`export.cljc:143-146`). The held-child row asserts the path (`lift_v1_test.cljc:181-183`) but that refusal comes from `enter`; the direct-lift child rows (`lift_v1_test.cljc:192-194`, `:210-213`) assert only the hold, honestly so — the data has no path. Not a contract breach (the ruling does not ask the lift to name a path), but an observability asymmetry worth one line in a later slice.

**3. (Low) The version-1 encode catch converts non-outcome throws into data.** `handoff.cljc:974-984`: any throw out of `jing.cbor/encode` that is not an S7.9 outcome becomes `:non-canonicalizable` with `(jing.cbor/refusal e)` — nil for a foreign exception — where the version-0 path (`(encode-bytes body)`, no catch) propagates defects. Jing's encoder is designed total-with-refusals, so the risk is theoretical defect-masking; noting the asymmetry, not asking for a change.

**4. (Low, coverage) The ffi-request and link-request variants lost their fixture pins.** The old successor base carried put/ffi-request/link-request frames with ids 0/1/2; the new one is a real parked writer with a single `:put` (`checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`, "successor" base). Consequently `checkpoint.cljc`'s per-variant `intent` mapping (`checkpoint.cljc:240-252`) and `protected!`'s `:ffi-request`/`:link-request` branches (`handoff.cljc:534-565`) are no longer exercised by any fixture or test — the baseline test now pins one `:put` op (`checkpoint_test.cljc:54-70`), and the protection rows are `:put`-only (`lift_v1_test.cljc:133-159`). This is ruling-conformant (the five accepted bases contain no retained ffi/link waiter, and the ruling's added rows do not name variants), but the drop is real; D11's fenced writer, or one lift-support machine parked on a retained call, should restore it.

**5. (Low, coverage) Three-level install trees are no longer fixture-pinned.** The old `installs` base carried a grandchild under `'bar`; the regenerated one is two-level, and `inherited_test`'s rebuilt `with-installs` grandchild is a hand-made clone for the authority's own purposes, not a lift. `tree-ops`'s depth-first recursion (`checkpoint.cljc:322-342`) is now unpinned past depth two. Ruling-conformant (the five named bases), noted for D11/D14.

**6. (Note) `emitted-version` removed.** The brief's wording notwithstanding, the ruling supersedes: a header means version 1, none means 0, and the headerless emission of 0 is pinned both against the direct 2-arity lift (`lift_v1_test.cljc:49-61`) and by the unmodified D7/D8 suites passing on three hosts. The removed var's own docstring said D9 raises it. No change needed; the engineer's one-line re-add offer is noted.

**7. (Note) `unliftable-hold` duplicates `refusal-at`.** `handoff.cljc:499-527` and `export.cljc:62-88` are line-for-line the same five-way cond (I compared: same order, same data). The dependency direction forces the copy (holder.export requires handoff), and both docstrings cross-reference the other slice, but the two chains can drift; a D10+ cleanup could push the predicate into handoff and have enter reuse it.

**8. (Carried, needs disposition) The document text owed** — UCF 7.7.4/7.8 (header as lift input, the self-check) and linker-dht 14.2.2 (enrolled set is a lift input and never travels). The ruling itself lists this text; the permitted diff names no doc files, and the engineer carries it exactly as D8 carried `:yin.k/refused`'s line. Acceptable, but it must not be lost: it should land with the architect's sign-off on this slice or as an immediate amendment.

**9. (Ruling on the widened diff — faithful, confirm the engineer's sign-off request)**
- `authority/inherited_test.cljc`, `authority/front_test.cljc`: the adaptations are faithful consequences. `carrying` and `inheriting` now clone the successor base's one write frame once per id where they used to index the old three-variant body; `with-installs` preserves the exact id topology the authority tests depend on (R's id 0 on the root, id 4 in the child, id 5 in a grandchild under `'bar`, counter 6), found by predicate (`frame-of`, `write-pending`) instead of by index, and the variant test's payload paths follow the same predicates. Cost: the three pendings are clones of one `:put` (finding 4) and the second install `'baz` is gone. No pinned cross-host contract was weakened — these are behavioral authority tests, all green on JVM and Dart.
- `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v1.txt`: a derived pin, correctly re-rendered. The C12 script offers bodies taken from `fx/fixtures "first-park"` and `"halted-root"` (`ledger_fixtures.cljc:67-84`), so the regeneration moves it. The diff is exactly 7 of 21 lines — the projection digest and six `a2` journal-value frames (the body-address-bearing ones); the magic and header lines are untouched; the new file is byte-identical to what `render` produces (size 32046 matches the regen log), no stale reference to any old digest or address survives anywhere in the repo (I grepped), and the three-lane proof still proves cross-host byte-determinism because the unchanged test structure is the proof: every host must re-render the file exactly (`the-file-is-the-rendered-script`), rebuild every frame byte-for-byte (`this-host-rebuilds-every-frame-byte-for-byte`), fold the pinned frames to the pinned digest over memory and file backends, and answer the documented script — run green on JVM, Node (full fast lane) and Dart (ledger-fixture) per the logs.

## The six attack areas, explicitly

**1. The self-check is load-bearing.** Before `:ok`, every version-1 root runs `checkpoint/inspect` over its own bytes and address (`handoff.cljc:986-989`) and every export — root, child, either version — runs `validate-body` over its own decoded bytes (`handoff.cljc:990`, unconditional), which itself recurses into install children (`:1294`). Children need no separate `inspect`: they carry no header (`export-installs` dissocs it, `:932`; the merge is guarded `child?`, `:972`), and the root's single inspect walks the whole tree in the root's context — child version agreement, kinds, `check-child-header!`, and every carried op id including duplicates (`checkpoint.cljc:322-342`). The refusals are data outcomes end to end: `call!` (`handoff.cljc:123-130`) converts only throws carrying the vocabulary, and every refusal path throws before the answer map, so `:bytes` is absent (pinned at `lift_v1_test.cljc:109-113` for the first-export halt; the same mechanism covers all others).

**2. Purity and the record.** `encode` reads only the machine and the record (`export.cljc:236-253`); its `serve-keyed` only reads the table, and the scratch ordering encoder (`canonical-order`, `handoff.cljc:240-255`) shares that same read-only serve, so ordering mints nothing. Zero serve calls in encode is transitively but genuinely pinned: `export_test.cljc:361-380`'s call counter is captured after prepare, survives two encodes and a re-prepare unchanged. Two encodes of one record give equal bytes on all three hosts (`lift_v1_test.cljc:229-240`, four machines including version 0); the record round-trips the canonical codec and re-encodes identically (`:273-281`); the served table is keyed `[task-path resource-id]` with the retained descriptor, the child path threads through (`:243-252`), one handle under two ids is served once and keyed twice (`:253-270` — the by-handle fix is real: `export.cljc:209-227` now enters the second key), and a removed key is still a defect. The `:op-seq-exhausted`, protection and header-kind checks precede encoding, so nothing is minted for a body that will refuse.

**3. The refusals, root and child.** `:op-seq-exhausted` at 2^52-1 by `num=` (`handoff.cljc:897-903`), `:unprotected-pending` naming the identity and `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream in root and child (`lift_v1_test.cljc:123-159`, the child rows real install children); a transient phase refuses `:incomplete-install` (`:162-168`, `handoff.cljc:710-712`); a first-export halt under a header is refused by the self-check with nothing answered (`:109-113`); a halted root with an origin emits origin only and passes both grammars (`:100-108`). Two readings I confirm as correct rather than gaps: `:op-seq-exhausted` fires on the root only, because the ruling's own kind/header agreement gives children no header and halted roots origin-only; and a counter *above* 2^52-1 is caught by the self-check as `:yin.k/undecodable :inexact` (`checkpoint.cljc:206-213`) rather than `:op-seq-exhausted` — the ruling names only 2^52-1, and the refusal is still data with nothing published. The nil-header path is byte-identical: the v0 found map carries no `:order` so `ordered` is the identity, the codec, version and body are unchanged, the nil-header test pins direct-lift equality (`lift_v1_test.cljc:49-61`), and the unmodified D7/D8 suites pin the 2-arity behavior on three hosts. The lift's hold refusals run before `lift-pending!` (`handoff.cljc:835-836`), in both versions, bypassing `enter` (`lift_v1_test.cljc:184-213`) — the D4/D5/D6 obligation is met.

**4. The widened diff** — ruled faithful in finding 9; it still needs the architect's nod the engineer asked for, which this review supports.

**5. The four cross-host fixes are portable and version-scoped as claimed.** The numeric-carrier branch (`handoff.cljc:294-305`) admits carriers exactly when `(:v1 @found)` and refuses `:host-object` with identical data to the old fall-through under version 0 — on the JVM nothing changes (Doubles are `scalar?`; BigDecimal/Ratio refused before and after), on JavaScript the Float64/Decimal/Rational carriers move from the `:else` refusal to the same refusal, under v1 they pass as-is into the jing body; the scratch encoder shares the admission so carrier keys sort. The opts grounding `(assoc (or opts {}) …)` (`handoff.cljc:843`) is the Dart assoc-on-nil fix, harmless elsewhere. `array-map` is replaced by `(into {} (reverse %))` with the `:cljd` gate and an honest degradation (`lift_v1_test.cljc:345-361`). The differs-check uses `cbor/content=` so Dart's `1 = 1.0` cannot pass `version-float` (`checkpoint_test.cljc`, `each-mutation-differs…`).

**6. The engineer's flagged items all check out.** The lift's hold refusals (above); the by-handle dedup fix (above); the D11 obligation recorded where the ruling wants it (`lift_support.cljc:284-296` docstring, plus the namespace docstring) including the regeneration trigger; the doc amendments carried (finding 8); and the lane scope honestly stated — Dart ran the eight affected namespaces, JVM the affected plus adjacent, and the brief itself names the aggregated landing lanes as the real proof.

