The header channel matches the ruling's argument in every particular: `prepare`'s fourth argument, `check-header!` throwing on the argument's shape only (`export.cljc:176-187` — the `set?` check covers a missing enrolled key), the header stored as `:header` beside `:served`, `encode` reading only machine and record, `header-of`'s kind selection with nil values dropped (`handoff.cljc:775-788`), `:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive` added by the lift, the enrolled set never traveling (asserted, `lift_v1_test.cljc:74`), and `prepare` minting nothing. The fixture regeneration meets every commitment: five real bases through enter/prepare/encode decoded from their own bytes, predicate-targeted mutations (including the two the partial diff still indexed), the two-thing mutation proof (differs at the named place; refusal path asserted), variants still successor mutations, `counter-at-bound` still a mutation the inspector accepts, the byte-level patch mechanism intact, addresses re-pinned once with the three anchors keeping full bytes (anchor digests match the cross-process digest log), the fixture name set unchanged, and the reader accepting all five bases (new row in `checkpoint_test.cljc`). The regenerate-on-every-run design (`the-file-holds-exactly-the-declared-bytes`) makes each host's test run a fresh determinism proof, which is what makes the three-lane claims credible without my rerunning anything.
